#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166512 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2936c86-e02f-5b66-8fe2-5aa81aab8754" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Slope Field of a Differential Equation at Some Isolated Points"}] :question/id #uuid "364c20d3-cb7d-5c14-b852-20943c6daf14" :question/math-academy-id "e-532" :question/problem "Sketch the slope field of the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 4x+2$ at the points indicated in the diagram above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3276/Source/Images/b687d982c8bad0481e67810405cd7841.png)" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the slope fields at the points $(-1,-2), (0,-2)$, and $(0,1)$.\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{dy}{dx} & _{(-1,-2)} = 4(-1) + 2 =-2 \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx} & _{(0,-2)} = 4(0) + 2 = 2 \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx} & _{(0, 1)} = 4(0) + 2 = 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3276/Source/Images/b2685fe9b87d7667afecf555d735d9ba.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166593 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3aa84ae8-6663-5aa1-844b-89090bcce735" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Slope Fields"}] :question/id #uuid "2b798d48-39fa-5b5c-8656-13e6a3829eb8" :question/math-academy-id "e-8500" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = x^2 + 2x$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. At every point along the line $x=-1$, the slope of the slope field does not vary\n2. At every point along the line $x=0$, the slope field is horizontal\n3. At every point along the line $y=1$, the slope of the slope field does not vary" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(x)\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $x$, the slope does not vary with $y$.\n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Consider a point of the form $(-1,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n$\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(-1,y)} = (-1)^2 + 2(-1) = -1$,\nand this implies that the slope of the slope field at the point $(-1, y)$ is equal to $-1$ for any $y$.\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(0,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n$\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(0,y)} = (0)^2 + 2(0) = 0$,\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(0,y)$ is horizontal for any $y$.\n- Statement III is false. Consider a point of the form $(x,1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n$\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,1)} = x^2 + 2x$,\nand this implies that the slope of the slope field at the point $(x,1)$ depends on $x$.\n\nWe can use the information given by the true statements to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3276/Source/Images/aed0e9885df5f5b12f09158f5a620d47.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166594 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5177f277-29a6-5df6-96c5-6bf53ffd9c51" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Slope Field Based on the Type of Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "a102dcd3-c75d-582e-93b9-208316a24bfb" :question/math-academy-id "e-8501" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(x)$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3276/Source/Images/30d15906f867b9b277b6ebf8b68c9455.png)" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(x)\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $x$, the slope field does not vary with $y$.\n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that does not vary with $y$ for a fixed value of $x$ is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3276/Source/Images/64636a65490baf0083defaad201186b7.png)\n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a vertical line, the slopes touching the line do not vary as we go up and down the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3276/Source/Images/e804b41c9a0fc8c7f90d69e11b991b1a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167388 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2936c86-e02f-5b66-8fe2-5aa81aab8754" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Slope Field of a Differential Equation at Some Isolated Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b066fdee-71a9-5e00-9e2f-a971c4b9d629" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3276/Source/Images/q-96083-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165877} {:answer/id #uuid "cf50a2c6-9952-5840-a241-59279e7315d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3276/Source/Images/q-96083-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165878} {:answer/id #uuid "3342424d-7816-502d-8c8b-264b580556de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3276/Source/Images/q-96083-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165879} {:answer/id #uuid "4bf7026b-8693-5133-b130-82326a6abe05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3276/Source/Images/q-96083-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165880} {:answer/id #uuid "6ce7a3a4-4220-5be7-af44-ea6805e3c82b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3276/Source/Images/q-96083-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165881}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4bf7026b-8693-5133-b130-82326a6abe05" :answer/t